EnableExplicit
Define a.w
Define b.w
Define r1.w
Define r2.w
Define r3.w
Define r4.w
Define r5.w
Define r6.w
Define r7.w
Define r8.w
Define r9.w
Define r10.w
a = 10
b = 3
r1 = a + b
r2 = a - b
r3 = a * b
r4 = 123 * 45
r5 = (5 + 3) * 2 - 6

; Division, which the 8080 and 6809 backends do not have at all. The Z80 has
; enough shift support for a restoring divide, so these are real.
r6 = 100 / 7
r7 = 100 % 7

; Signed cases. The core loop is unsigned and the signs are reapplied after, so
; these are what catches a missing sign fixup. Truncation is toward zero, so
; -100 / 7 is -14 (not -15) and the remainder takes the DIVIDEND's sign.
r8 = -100 / 7
r9 = -100 % 7
r10 = 100 / -7

; Divide by zero. Deliberately no special case: with a zero divisor the
; "remainder < divisor" test is never true, so every quotient bit gets set and
; the routine returns -1 with the remainder equal to the dividend. That is
; exactly what the AVR backend was MEASURED to do on silicon, so the two targets
; agree by construction. Going through a variable so the frontend cannot fold it.
Define z.w
Define r11.w
Define r12.w
z = 0
r11 = 100 / z
r12 = 100 % z
